Build Blast on Linux with a normal GCC (build.sh --no-docker) instead of
NVIDIA's GCC 9 container. Tested with GCC 13 and 16.

Newer GCC warns a lot on Blast and it builds with warnings as errors, so
those stay warnings now. Three files were missing includes. The abi flag
Blast uses breaks unique_ptr on GCC 13, -fnew-inheriting-ctors fixes that.
The tests need -no-pie. And Blast casts between its vector types, which
optimized GCC builds don't expect, so fracture authoring just hangs without
-fno-strict-aliasing. MSVC never optimizes that way, so it's fine on Windows.

diff --git a/blast/premake5.lua b/blast/premake5.lua
index 49422db..61c346a 100644
--- a/blast/premake5.lua
+++ b/blast/premake5.lua
@@ -209,6 +209,9 @@ workspace (workspace_name)
         symbols "On"
 
         buildoptions { "-pthread -fvisibility=hidden -fnon-call-exceptions -D_FILE_OFFSET_BITS=64 -fabi-version=8" }
+        -- For newer GCC: warnings don't fail the build, Blast casts between its vector
+        -- types (breaks strict aliasing), and the abi flag above breaks unique_ptr on GCC 13.
+        buildoptions { "-Wno-error", "-fno-strict-aliasing", "-fnew-inheriting-ctors" }
         -- enforces RPATH instead of RUNPATH, needed for ubuntu version > 16.04
         linkoptions { "-pthread", 
                       "-Wl,--no-undefined",
@@ -594,6 +597,8 @@ group "tests"
             -- defines { "ISOLATION_AWARE_ENABLED=1" }
         filter { "system:linux" }
             buildoptions { "-fPIC" }
+            -- GCC builds PIE by default now.
+            linkoptions { "-no-pie" }
             links { "rt" }
         filter{}
 
